{"causes": ["The foundational military and administrative consolidation by his father, Samudragupta, provided a stable and expansive empire for Chandragupta II to inherit.", "The strategic location and rich agricultural lands of the Ganges Plain offered the Gupta Empire a strong economic base for expansion and cultural patronage.", "The decline of the Kushan Empire in the northwest created a power vacuum, allowing the Guptas to expand their influence and control over key trade routes."], "effects": ["The absorption of the Saka Western Satraps territories under Chandragupta II opened lucrative trade routes to the West, significantly boosting the Gupta economy.", "The patronage of arts and sciences during his reign led to the 'Golden Age' of India, fostering advancements in literature, mathematics, astronomy, and metallurgy.", "Chandragupta II's successful rule solidified the Gupta Empire's reputation as a dominant regional power, inspiring subsequent Hindu kingdoms and cultural developments."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "samudragupta", "sourceName": "Samudragupta", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "chandragupta-ii", "targetName": "Chandragupta II", "context": "Samudragupta's extensive military conquests and administrative reforms established the powerful Gupta Empire that Chandragupta II inherited and further expanded."}, {"sourceSlug": "chandragupta-ii", "sourceName": "Chandragupta II", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "pataliputra", "targetName": "Pataliputra, India", "context": "Chandragupta II ruled his vast Gupta Empire primarily from its capital city of Pataliputra (modern Patna) during his reign from 375 CE to 415 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "chandragupta-ii", "sourceName": "Chandragupta II", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "kalidasa", "targetName": "Kalidasa", "context": "Chandragupta II's court is traditionally associated with the patronage of the classical Sanskrit poet and dramatist Kalidasa, whose works represent a zenith of Indian literature."}, {"